    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d445">

                <pb facs="0410" n="294" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg294"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 351.117/6.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Ambassador Herrick to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 509.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-01-19">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 19, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your telegram of December 27,
                    3 p.m., and my despatch No. 494 of January 2, 1914, in regard to the arrest of
                    Mr. Joseph Nouguier, for non-performance of his military service, I have the
                    honor to report that in accordance with your telegraphic instructions of January
                    16, 2 p.m., I have presented his case to the Foreign Office and requested that
                    it be made possible for him to leave France and return to America.</p>
                <p>I will cable the Department the result of my representations as soon as an answer
                    is received from the Foreign Office.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Myron T. Herrick</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
